 <iati-activity default-currency="EUR" hierarchy="1" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>NL-1-PPR-23447</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-7" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the Netherlands</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BAGC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">All 14 investees were found to be applying their equity and loan capital responsibly, resulting in considerable expansion. Expansion occurred in three forms: i) linear growth of current activities; ii) expansion into downstream activities (e.g. from maize trader to maize processing); and iii) diversification into new activities such as potato and seed production. Expansion, however, did not come easy as many of the protagonist investors came from farming backgrounds with poor management and accounting skills. Business development services via third party consultants that were meant to help investees in terms of management capacity building and technical advice came only late into the intervention. Perhaps the most important development impact was the nurturing of pioneering activities which are likely to influence the further expansion of these activities including: large scale potato cultivation, fully integrated seed production, fully integrated production of pork meat and the introduction of a goat slaughterhouse dependent on aggregator collection of live animals. Perhaps equally important was the development of innovative activities including: the introduction of a new and critical avocado variety which has now been planted on a very large scale in the province; improved litchi production methods that have doubled yields; improved honey production methods; the introduction of the country&#x2019;s first maize out-grower scheme; and the introduction of soya milk yoghurt.</narrative>
  </description>
